Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh, Manasseh the father of Amon, Amon the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiachin and his brothers. And in that time, the Jews were forced to go to Babylon.
And after that, the family lineage continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azur was the father of Sadok. Sadok was the father of Aqueem. Aqueem was the father of Eleuode.
Eliab was the father of Eliezer. Eliezer was the father of Mattenai. Mattenai was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph, and Joseph was the husband of Mary, and Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
So there were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 generations from David until the Jews were forced to leave for Babylon. And there were 14 generations from that time to Christ. And Jesus Christ was born
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about: His mother Mary was pledged to be married to Joseph. Before they came together, it was found that she was with child. The child had been conceived by the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a pious man. He did not want to shame her in front of people. Therefore, he decided to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary as your wife, for the child who is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son, and you shall name him Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins.
All of this happened in order for what God said would happen to come true. God said this through the prophet:
The virgin will conceive a child. She will give birth to a son, and he will be called Immanuel." (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us".
Joseph took Mary to be his wife, as the angel of the Lord had commanded him in a dream. And Joseph did not know her until she had given birth to her firstborn son, and he called him Jesus.
But he did not consummate the marriage until she bore her son, and he named him Joseph: Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They said, "Where is the child who has been born to be king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
And whoever heard this thing about the king Herod, was very much upset. And everything in Jerusalem was also very upset.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
They answered, "In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written through the prophet:
"And you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, yet out of you will come one who will be a ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5: 2)
And after that, Herod secretly called the wise men. And he asked them about the exact time when the star appeared.
And he sent them to Bethlehem, saying, "Go and search carefully for the child, and when you find him, report to me so that I may also go and worship him."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they continued their journey. And the star which they had seen when they were in the east, went before them. And when they had come to the house, they saw the young child with Mary his mother, and fell down, and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures, and presented him with gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
And when they saw the star, they were very happy.
The wise men walked to the house. And they saw there the boy with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. And after that they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. Jesus' family flees to Egypt
After the wise men had gone, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream. The angel said to him, "Get up, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return." When Herod realized that he had been outwitted by the Magi, he was furious. He gave
Joseph got up. And during the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
And they remained there until the death of King Herod. And so, the word of the Lord, which He spoke through the prophet, was fulfilled: "Out of Egypt I have called my son." (Hosea 11: 1)
And Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued his orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the young boys who were two years old or less were to be killed. This was in accordance with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
And thus the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said:
A voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation and great weeping. Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted for her children because they are not. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while still in Egypt. And in the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother and go to the land of Israel, for those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
So Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But afterwards he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. Instead, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee after receiving a warning in a dream.
And he lived there in a city called Nazareth. And so it happened that what was spoken by the prophets about Jesus came true, for they said: "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist paves the way.
In those days John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothing was made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
And people came to Him from Jerusalem and all Judea and from the region beyond the Jordan.
And when they confessed that they had sinned, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Bring forth fruit, then, demonstrating that you have repented of your sins.
Do not think that you can say to yourselves: Abraham is our father. I am telling you: God can raise up children to Abraham even from these stones.
The axe is already at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repentance for your sins. But after me will come one who is mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire."
He has a pitchfork in his hand to rake the hay from his barn. He will thresh his wheat in the granary. But he will burn the chaff with unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why do you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now, for it is proper for us to do this, because it fulfills the holy plan of God." And John agreed.
And no sooner had Jesus been baptized than he came up out of the water. And at that moment the heavens were opened. And Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove upon him.
And a voice came from heaven, saying, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. And there, Satan tempted him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of not eating, Jesus felt hungry.
And the tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
And after that, the devil took Jesus to the holy city, and they stood on the highest point of the temple.
He said to him: "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone,'" (Deuteronomy 6: 16).
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only' " (Deuteronomy 6:13).
And after that, the devil moved away from Jesus. And the angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to call people.
John had been imprisoned. When Jesus heard this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the town of Capernaum, which was located beside the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And so, what the prophet Isaiah said came true. For he said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! The Galilee, where non-Jews live! The land that lies along the Mediterranean Sea! The region east of the Jordan River!
The people who are now living in the darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9: 1, 2)
And from that time, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
Jesus was once walking by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets at once and followed him.
From there, he saw two other disciples, James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in the boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets. Jesus called them.
They left the boat and their father at once and followed Jesus. Jesus healed the sick.
Jesus went throughout all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the good news of the kingdom of God. And he was healing all the diseases and sicknesses that were among the people.
News of him spread throughout Syria. People were coming to him with everyone who was sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pains. Others were under the control of demons. Some were trembling violently. Others were unable to move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
Great crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region across the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds, so he went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
And then he started teaching them. He said:
Blessed are those who need spirituality. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for justice, for they will be satisfied.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer to do good. Theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people revile you and persecute you on my account. Blessed are you even when people say all kinds of evil against you on my account.
Be happy and pleased. Your reward in the sky is great. People always do the same thing, harming the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But what will happen if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it become salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will walk over it and trample it.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but rather puts it on a lampstand, so that those who come in may see the light.
All the people don't light a candle and put it under the bowl. But they put it on top of the fireplace of the house. And so the whole house is illuminated by everything that is in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gave a perfect meaning to the law.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the law or the prophets; I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them.
What I am going to tell you now is true. Heaven and earth shall pass away, but not an iota or one tittle of the law shall in any wise pass away, until all be fulfilled.
Do not break even the least of these commandments. And do not teach people to break them. If you do this, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. But, obey these commandments and teach them. Then you will be called important people in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you: You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and the teachers of the law. Otherwise, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
You have heard what was said to the people who lived a long time ago. It was said to them: "You shall not murder." (Exodus 20:13) Everyone who murders will be put on trial for it.
But this is what I tell you: Don't be angry with your brother. If anyone is angry with their brother, they will be brought to judgment. And anyone who says to their brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' will be brought to judgment before the Sanhedrin. But anyone who says, 'You idiot!' will be in danger of fiery Gehenna. (Note: I have translated "أحمق" as "good-for-nothing" and "غبي" as "idiot". These are arguably stronger terms than the original Arabic, but they aim to capture the intended insult conveyed in the original text. A more literal translation of "أحمق" might be "fool" or "imbecile", while "غبي" could be translated as "stupid" or "brainless". However, I believe the chosen translations effectively convey the intended meaning and severity of the insults within the original Arabic text.)
Assume that you are presenting your offering at the altar. And remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother, and then come back and offer your gift.
If someone has a claim against you and wants to take you to court, settle this matter quickly. Do this while you are still together on your way. If you do not do this, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the policeman. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I'm about to tell you is true. You're not leaving until you pay the last cent! Marital infidelity
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But I tell you: whoever looks at a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. For your eye is not a part of your body, but rather a part of your body. It is better for you to lose it than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is only a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, 'Suppose a man puts away his wife. If he does that, he must give her a certificate of divorce.' (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you: Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man cannot divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. Oaths
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. It was said to them, "Do not break the covenants you have made with the Lord your God. You shall not break your solemn vows, which you have made to him for the performance of good deeds. For you have freely sworn to the Lord your God, with your own mouth, which the Lord your God had not commanded you to do, that you might not sin by doing such things as the Lord your God would certainly not have commanded you to do."
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't make any promises like that at all. Don't give them in the name of heaven. That's God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the Earth. For this is the seat on which God places His feet. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. For this is the city of the great King.
And don't swear by your heads. You are not able to change even a single hair to white or to black.
Let your "yes" mean "yes," and let your "no" mean "no." Anything more than this comes from the evil one. Be kind to one another.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21).
But this is what I tell you: Do not resist an evil person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, turn to him the other cheek also.
If we assume that someone takes you to court to take your shirt, let him also take your jacket.
If someone compels you to go one mile, go with him two miles.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not turn away from someone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:18) and "hate your enemy."
But this is what I tell you: love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.
Here is the translation: And with this thing, you will be sons of your father, up to the sky.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
If you are only exchanging greetings with your brothers, what is the merit you have over others? Even the disbelievers do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Providing assistance to people in need.
Be careful not to do your righteous acts before people, so that they may see you. If you do that, your Father who is in heaven will not reward you.
If you are charitable to those in need, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like the hypocrites, who ostentatiously display their piety. They proclaim their good deeds in the synagogues and on the streets, so that people may praise them. Truly I tell you, they have already received their full reward.
If you give something to someone in need, don't let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving should be secret after that. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer.
They do not pray if they are going to be like those people who are pretending to be pious. They like to stop and pray in the mosques and in the streets so that people can see them. It is true that what I am going to tell you now is this: they took their full reward
If you pray, go into your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who is unseen. And He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret.
If you are praying, do not babble meaninglessly as the pagans do, for they think that they will be heard because of their many words.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Come to your senses. What you wanted will be done, what is expected will be done on earth as it is done in heaven.
Give us today our daily bread.
Forgive us our sins, as we also forgive those who sin against us.
We have preserved those whom we would not let fall into sin, since we were experimenting. We have saved those from the devil, who is evil.
Excuse people if they have wronged you. If you do that, your father will wrap the sky and will forgive you too.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
If you are fasting and not eating, do not be gloomy like the hypocrites who pretend to be righteous. They turn their faces to look very sad, to show people that they are fasting. Truly I tell you: they have received their full reward.
But when you don't eat, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
And so, the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your father, who cannot be seen, will know this. And he will reward you. Your father sees what is done in secret. Set your wealth aside in heaven.
Do not gather up treasures on earth for yourselves, where moth and rust consume, and where thieves break in and steal.
Hide treasures for yourselves in heaven, where thieves do not break in, nor are they stolen.
Your heart will be wherever your treasures are.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Suppose your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But let's suppose your eyes are bad. Your whole body will be filled with darkness. If the light within you is darkness, it means the darkness is intense!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. They will hate one and love the other, or they will be loyal to one and refuse the other. You cannot serve both God and money at the same time. Don't think
«I tell you: Do not worry. Do not worry about your life, about what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body, about what you will wear. Is not life more than food? And is not the body more than clothing?»
Look at the birds of the sky. They do not sow, nor do they gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they?
Can your worrying add even one more hour to your life?
"And why do you care about clothing? Look at how wildflowers grow. They neither toil nor spin."
But that's what I'm telling you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like these lilies.
If God clothes wild grass this way, won't He clothe you even better? For the grass is here today only. And tomorrow it is thrown into the fire. Your faith is very weak!
Therefore, do not be anxious, and do not say, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
People without faith run after all these things. Your father in heaven knows that you need them.
But first seek the kingdom of God, and do His will, and all these things shall be added unto you.
Therefore, do not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard by which you judge others. You will be evaluated by the same measure that you use to evaluate others.
You see only the little bit of sawdust in your friend's eye, but you do not see the beam that is in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me take the tiny piece of wood out of your eye"? How can you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
Hypocrite! First, take the log out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the splinter out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before the swine. If you do that, they may trample them under their feet. Then they may turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Seek and you shall be given, search and you shall find, knock and the door shall be opened for you.
Everyone who seeks will receive. Whoever searches will find. The door will be opened for the one who knocks.
Imagine that your son asked for bread. Which one of you would give him a stone?
Or let's suppose he asked for a fish. Which one of you would give him a snake?
And if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what was written in the Law and by the Prophets. The large and small gates
Enter through the narrow gate, for wide is the gate and broad is the road that leads to destruction, and many are those who are on it.
But the gate is narrow and the road leading to life is difficult, and few are those who find it. The tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know them by what they do. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thorny patches?"
In the same way, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear bad fruit. And a bad tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven. For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is the one who will enter the kingdom of heaven.
On that day many will say to me, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then I will openly say to them, "I never knew you; depart from me, you evildoers!" The wise and discerning, and the foolish.
"So, everyone who hears my words and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain falls. The water rises. The winds blow and they strike this house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not put them into practice is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
It's raining. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it falls with a loud sound of breaking.
And when Jesus had finished all these words, the crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as if he had authority. He did not speak as their teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain. And great multitudes followed him.
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came, and he fell on his knees before Jesus. And he said, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me 'clean.' "
Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I want to do this. 'Be clean!'" So the man was immediately healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded. You will be a witness to them." A Roman officer believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus to help him.
He said, "Oh Lord, my servant is lying in the house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
And the centurion answered and said, "Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest enter under my roof: but speak the word only, and my servant shall be healed."
I myself am a man subject to authority. And I have soldiers who obey my commands. I say to this one, 'Go,' and he goes. And I say to that one, 'Come,' and he comes. I say to my servant, 'Do this,' and he does it."
And when Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who were following him, "Truly I say to you, I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel."
I will tell you that many will come from the East and the West. They will take their places at the feast of the Kingdom of Heaven. They will sit with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But the people who believe they belong to the kingdom will be thrown outside, into the darkness. And there they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman officer, "Go! It will be just as you believed it would." And the man's servant was healed at that very hour. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed, suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her with his hand, and the fever left her. She got up and began to serve him.
And when evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. And he cast out the spirits with a word, and he healed all who were sick.
This happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet Isaiah: "He went down to the depths of the underworld, as far as the gates of death; but he did not enter into them, for it is said of him: 'His suffering will atone for our sins, and his torment will make us whole.' " (Isaiah 53:4-5). Following Jesus costs.
Jesus saw the crowd that was surrounding him, and he gave orders to his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have dens and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
And another disciple said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm
Jesus got into a boat. His disciples followed him.
And suddenly, a strong windstorm blew on the lake. And the waves began to beat the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples went down and woke him up. They said to him, "Master, save us! We are perishing!"
He answered them, "It is because you have so little faith! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves. The storm stopped and all was calm.
The students were amazed. They asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus healed two men who were under the control of demons.
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. There he encountered two men who were possessed by demons. They were coming from the cemetery, and the two men were so violent that no one could pass by that way.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come here to punish us before it is time for our trial?"
Not far from there, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you are going to drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" And the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. Then the herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned in the water.
And those who had been taking care of the pigs fled. And they went to the city and reported everything. And they told the people what had happened to the men who had been under the control of the demons.
Then the whole city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who was not able to walk.
Jesus got into a boat. And he crossed over to the other side of the lake, and he came to his own city.
They brought to Him some men who carried a man who could not walk. He was sleeping on a mat. Jesus saw that they had faith. And He said to the man: Do not lose hope, my son. Your sins will be forgiven.
And after that, some jurists of the law said among themselves: This man is saying something very bad.
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said, "Why do you think evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise up and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." And after this, he spoke to the man who could not walk. He said to him, "Get up, pick up your mat, and go to your house."
The man got up and went home.
And when the crowd saw this, they were amazed and glorified God, who had given such authority to people. Jesus Chooses Matthew
Jesus went away from there, and saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And rising up, Matthew followed him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this, and they asked Jesus' disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this and said, "It is not those who are well who need a physician, but those who are ill."
Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice.' For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day." Then he went back to the synagogue. And a man was there whose hand was withered. And they asked him, saying, "Is
One day, the disciples of John came to him and said to Jesus: "John and the Pharisees fast; why do not your disciples fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom is taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not sew a new patch of cloth onto old garments. The new patch would tear away from the old. This would make the tear even worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins. If they did, the wineskins would burst. The wine would be lost, and the wineskins would be ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. This way, both are preserved - the two: a dead girl and a suffering woman.
And while Jesus was saying this, one of the rulers came. And he knelt down before Jesus. And he said, "My daughter has just now died. But come and lay your hand on her, and she will live."
Jesus stood up and went with him, and so did his disciples.
At that time, a woman came from behind Jesus. She had been suffering from a disease that caused her to bleed. This disease had been with her for 12 years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
I thought: I only need to touch his clothes, and I will be healed.
Dar Issa and saw her. He said to her: "Beloved woman, do not lose hope. Your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed at that moment.
And what does Jesus have to do with the governor's house? When he saw there were flute players there, he saw the crowded and noisy audience.
Go, the girl is not dead, but sleeping, but they mocked him.
After the crowd was sent away, Jesus went in and took the girl by the hand, and she got up.
The news about what Jesus had done spread throughout the region. Jesus healed two blind men.
And while Jesus was going out from there, two blind men followed him. And they cried out, saying, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus went inside, the blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They answered him, "Yes, sir."
Then he touched their eyes and said, "What you believed in will happen to you."
And they could see again. And Jesus warned them severely, saying, "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. And they talked about it all over the region.
And as they were coming out, they brought to Jesus another man who had a demon. This demon had seized him, and he could not speak.
When the devil had gone out, the man spoke. And the crowd was astonished. And they said, "We have never seen anything like this in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, 'He casts out demons by the prince of demons.' And the number of workers is so small!
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. He was teaching in their synagogues. He was preaching the good news of the kingdom. And he was healing all manner of diseases and ailments.
When he saw that many people were gathered, he was moved by concern for them. They were humiliated and had nothing to help them, they resembled sheep without a shepherd.
And afterward, Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is abundant, but the laborers are few."
Therefore, ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Following them are Philip and Bartholomew, as well as Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. There are two others: James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
And their last was Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. And Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with the following instructions: "Do not go among the Gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any town of the Samaritans."
Go rather to the house of Israel, for they are like lost sheep.
Go and tell them, "The kingdom of heaven is near."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Make those with skin diseases "clean" again. Drive out demons. You received freely, so give freely.
Do not carry with you any gold, silver, or copper in your wallets.
Do not take any luggage with you when you travel. Do not take any extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The employee should only take what is needed.
If you enter a city or a village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's house until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet the people who live there.
If that house welcomes you, give it the blessing of peace. And if it is not so, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not do so, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or that city.
What I am about to tell you is true. On the Day of Judgment, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for this city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Pay attention! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
They will bring you before governors and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for those who are not Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it, for at that time you will be given the appropriate words to say.
It will not be you who speaks. It is your father's spirit that will speak through you.
The brother will betray his brother to be killed. The father will betray his children. The son will rebel against his parents and will let them be killed.
They hate you all because of me. But whoever will have patience until the end will be saved.
If people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not finish visiting the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
No student is better than his teacher. No servant is better than his masters.
It is sufficient for a disciple to become like his teacher. And it is sufficient for a slave to become like his master. If the master of the house has been named Baal Zebub, how much more will the members of his household be called by that name?
Therefore, do not fear your enemies. For everything that is secret will be revealed, and everything that is hidden will be made known.
What I tell you in the dark, speak of it in the light. What whispers in your ear, shout it from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the spirit. Rather, fear the one who can destroy both body and soul in hell.
"Or two sparrows for a penny? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground outside your Father's knowledge."
He even numbers the hairs of your head!
So, don't be afraid. You are worth more than many sparrows.
As for those who say in front of others that they know me, what about that? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about the person who says in front of others that he does not know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I do not know him.
"Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make "sons rebel against their fathers. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. Daughters-in-law will rebel against their mothers-in-law.
"The enemies of man are the people of his house" (Micah 7:6).
Anyone who loves his father or his mother more than me is not worthy of me. Anyone who loves his son or his daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
He who finds his life will lose it. And he who loses his life because of me will find it.
"Anyone who welcomes you welcomes me. And anyone who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me."
If someone receives a prophet as a prophet, that person will receive the reward of a prophet. And if someone receives a righteous person as a righteous person, that person will receive the reward of a righteous person.
Imagine if someone gave even a cup of cold water to a little child following me. What I am going to tell you now is a fact. This person will surely be rewarded."
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus completed the teaching of his 12 disciples. After that, he went to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
Solo Isa: Are you the one who was supposed to come? Or do we need to wait for someone else?
Jesus answered them, "Go back to John and tell him what you hear and see.
Blind people can see. Disabled people can walk. People with skin diseases are cured. Deaf people can hear. Dead people come back to life. And good news is told to poor people.
Blessed are those who do not leave their faith for my sake.
And when John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
Otherwise, what have you come out to see? Someone in fine clothing? No. People who dress in fine clothing live in the kings' palaces.
So, what did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one concerning whom it was written in the Holy Book. It says: "I will send My messenger ahead of you. He will prepare your way" (Malachi 3:1).
What I am going to tell you right now is true. No one greater than John the Baptist has ever been born. But even the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace. Isaiah 9:6-7
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
He who has ears, let him hear.
Who can I compare the people of today with? They are like children sitting in the marketplaces, calling out to others, saying:
"We played a tune on the reed pipe for you, but you did not dance. We sang a funeral dirge, but you were not grieved."
When John came, he didn't eat or drink like you. And people say: He's got a jinn.
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank with you. And they say, 'This man is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' But wisdom is vindicated by her deeds.' The cities that did not repent.
Jesus began to speak against the cities where He had done most of His miracles, because the people there had not repented of their sins. And He said:
"Woe to you, Chorazin! And woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago, sitting in sackcloth and ashes."
But I tell you this: it will be better for Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you.
And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No, but you will be brought down to the place of the dead. For if the miracles had been performed in Sodom which were performed in you, it would have remained to this day.
But I tell you this: The day of reckoning will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who are weary.
Jesus said at that time: "I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to children.
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
"My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to introduce to him."
Come to me all of you who are weary and carry heavy burdens, and I will give you rest.
Be my servants and learn from me, for I am gentle and without pride. You will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry, so they began to pick the heads of wheat and eat them.
The Freisians saw this and said to Jesus, "Look! This is forbidden to do on the Sabbath, but your disciples are doing it!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did when he and his men were hungry?
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. He only allowed the priests to eat it.
Have you not read the law? It says how every Sabbath day the priests in the temple must do their service on that day. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that he who is greater than the temple is here.
The Holy Book says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
And from after that place, Jesus went to their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were looking for a reason to accuse Jesus, so they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Would you not seize it and pull it out?"
A man is worth more than a sheep! That's why the law allows us to do good deeds on the Sabbath.
And after that, Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it became sound and whole, like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus was aware of all the schemes of the Pharisees. Therefore, He left that place. Many followed Him, and He healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell anyone who he was.
This was to fulfill what had been spoken by the prophet Isaiah. For he said:
"Here is my servant whom I have chosen, the one in whom I delight. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will proclaim justice to the nations: a light for the revelation of all things."
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a weak flame that is burning. He will repair everything.
The nations shall put their hope in him.
They brought to Jesus a man who was being controlled by demons. This man was blind and could not speak. Jesus healed him. After that, the man could speak and see.
All the people were amazed and were saying, "Can this be the Son of David?"
And the Pharisees heard this, and they said, "This man casts out demons by the prince of demons, Beelzebul!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself will not stand.
If Satan drives out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You keep saying, "This one drives out demons by the power of Beelzebul!" And why didn't you say, "By whose power do your people drive them out?" Those will be the ones to judge you.
But suppose I drive out demons by the Spirit of God. Then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's home and take only what the man possesses? You must first bind him. Then you can rob his house.
Everyone who is not with me is against me. And everyone who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
Therefore, this is what I say to you: Every sin and every blasphemous word that is spoken against God will be forgiven. But the blasphemous word against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Anyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But anyone who speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. Such a person will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you plant a good tree, its fruit will be good. And if you plant a bad tree, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
"O nest of poisonous vipers! How is it possible for you, who are evil, to say anything good? Your mouths speak all that is in your hearts."
A good man speaks good words. This comes from the goodness he hides in his heart. A bad man speaks bad words. This comes from the evil he hides in his heart.
But that's what I'm telling you. On the day of reckoning, people will have to answer for every careless word they spoke.
You will be condemned or acquitted based on your words. The sign of Yunus, the miracle.
And some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus and said to him, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered, "Evil and faithless people demand a miraculous sign! But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a large fish for three days and three nights. Such a thing will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will stand up on the day of judgment with the people who are living now. And the men of Nineveh will prove that those people are guilty. The men of Nineveh repented of their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now there is someone more important than Jonah who is here.
The Queen of the South will stand up on the day of judgment with the people who are living now, and will prove that they are guilty. She came from a very far place to listen to the wisdom of Solomon. And now there is someone more important than Solomon here.
What happens when the evil spirit leaves the man? It goes to dry places to look for a place to rest. But it doesn't find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned and tidied.
Then the unclean spirit goes and takes with itself seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter and dwell there. And the man's state becomes worse than it was before. Thus will be the fate of the wicked in this time. The mother of Jesus and his brothers.
And while Jesus was speaking to the crowds, his mother and brothers were standing outside. They wanted to speak to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother, your siblings, they are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
Jesus answered and said to him, "Who is my mother? And who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "This is my mother! And these are my brothers!"
Anyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On that day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the Sea of Galilee.
A great crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people stood on the beach.
And after that, he told them many things through stories. He said: There was a farmer who went out to sow his seeds.
Sow the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there was not much soil. Plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They withered because they did not have roots.
And as for the other seeds, they fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
And there were other seeds that fell on good soil. And they produced a crop that was a hundred or sixty or thirty times greater than what was sown.
He who has ears to hear, let him hear.
The students came to him. They asked him, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "You have been given a chance to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. This opportunity has not been given to outsiders."
To everyone who has this kind of knowledge, more will be given; in fact, they will have a great deal. But for those who do not have this kind of knowledge, even the little they have will be taken away.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they do not really see. They listen, but they do not really hear or understand."
In them are fulfilled the words of the prophet Isaiah, who said: "You will hear but never understand; you will see but never perceive what you see."
And the hearts of these people have become callous; they hardly hear with their ears, and they have closed their eyes, lest they see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and return and be healed." (Isaiah 6: 9,10)
But blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
What I am going to tell you now is true. Many prophets and righteous people desired to see what you are seeing. But they did not see it. They desired to hear what you are hearing. But they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then, the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like a seed planted on a path.
And others received the seeds that fell on the rocks. These are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. Therefore, they last only for a short time. They quickly fall away from the faith when troubles or suffering come because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. They prevent it from bearing fruit.
But others received the seeds that fell on fertile ground. These are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop that is 100, 60, or 30 times more than what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told the crowds another parable. He said, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven. A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then went away.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him and said, 'Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?'
"This is what an enemy has done," he answered. The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner answered. "For while you are pulling up the weeds, you may also root up the wheat."
Let them grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the reapers, "Gather the weeds first, and bind them into bundles to be burned; but gather the wheat into my barn."' The Parables of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. A man took it and planted it in his field.
Here is the smallest seed from all of your seeds. However, when it grows bigger, it becomes the largest plant in the whole garden. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast, which a woman took and mixed with a large amount of flour, and the dough became leavened."
Jesus used to tell all these things to the people through parables. He was not saying anything to them without a parable.
And so the words spoken by the prophet were fulfilled, for he had said, "I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter dark sayings from of old." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explained the parable of the weeds.
And after that, Jesus went away from the people and entered the house. And his disciples came to him, and they said to him: Explain to us the parable of the herb that is found in the field.
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. And the harvest is the day of judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are being pulled up and burned in the fire. It will be like this on the Day of Judgment.
Gerardi, the human being, was created by angels. And they will purify his kingdom of everything that causes sin. And they will remove all that the evil ones do.
They will throw them into hell, where the fire burns. There they will cry and gnash their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine forth like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Let him who has ears listen. Tales of the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl
"The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. A man found it and hid it again. Then he went off to sell all that he had. And he bought that field."
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant looking for fine pearls.
One of them found a pearl of great value. So he went and sold everything he owned, and bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. This was thrown into the lake. And it caught all kinds of fish.
And when it was full, the fishermen drew it to shore. And sitting down, they gathered the good fish into baskets. But throwing the bad fish away.
Thus it will be on the Day of Resurrection. The angels will come. And they will separate those who did evil from those who did good.
They will unleash them upon the people, the worst of them, in that furnace which is being ignited. There, the worst of them will weep, and their teeth will chatter.
Do you understand all this? Jesus asked them. Yes, they answered him.
He said to them, "To what can I compare the people of this generation? They are like children sitting in the marketplace. One calls out to another, saying, 'We played the flute for you, but you did not dance. We sang a mournful song, but you did not weep.' For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you say, 'He has a demon.' The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and you say, 'Here is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' But wisdom is justified by her deeds." A Prophet Without Honor
And when Jesus had finished these parables, he departed thence.
And he came to his hometown Nazareth. And there he began to teach people in their synagogue. And they were amazed. And they said, "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to perform miracles?"
"Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother called Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?"
Isn't all his sisters with us? Where did this man get all this from?
They were never satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them: "A prophet is not without honor, except in his own country, and in his own house."
Very few miracles happened there because they did not believe.
John the Baptist was beheaded.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and the adjoining territory, heard about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Philip, the brother of Herod.
John was saying to Herod, "You have no right to marry her, because it is against the law."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He swore that he would give her everything she asked of him.
Her mother told her what she should say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very upset. But he remembered his promise and his guests whom he had invited for dinner. So, he ordered one of his men to give her what she had asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and delivered to the girl. She then carried it to her mother.
And John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand people
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he got into a boat and went to a quiet place. But the people heard about it and they followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came ashore, he saw a great crowd. He was filled with compassion for them. He healed their sick.
And when evening came, his disciples came to him and said, "This place is deserted, and it is already late. Send the crowd away to go to the villages and buy for themselves food."
Jesus answered, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
They answered, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said, "Bring them here."
And after that, Jesus told the people to sit down on the grass. He took the five loaves of bread and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and blessed God. He broke the loaves of bread into pieces. And then he gave them to his disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. And the disciples gathered twelve baskets full of the leftover fragments.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
And Jesus immediately instructed his disciples to get into the boat and go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he dismissed the crowd.
And after he had dismissed them, he went up to the mountain to pray by himself. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far from the shore. The waves were hitting it because the wind was blowing against it.
In the early morning, Jesus went to the disciples. And he walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were frightened. They said, "This is a ghost!" and screamed in fear.
Jesus immediately called out to them, saying, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid!"
"Lord, is it you?" Peter asked. "If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." And Peter, leaving the boat, walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Jesus immediately reached out his hand, took hold of him, and said to him, "O you of little faith, why did you doubt?"
When they got to the boat, the wind had died down.
And after that, those who were in the boat worshipped him. And they said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
And they crossed the lake and reached Gennesaret.
The men who used to live there recognized Jesus. So they sent messengers to the surrounding countryside. And people brought all their sick to Jesus.
They asked him to allow the sick to touch only the edge of his robe. And everyone who touched it was healed.
What makes people impure?
And some Pharisees and experts in the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked him:
Why don't your students, the ones taught by the Sheikhs, apply what they've learned? Your students don't wash their hands before eating.
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the commandment of God? But you prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You nullify the word of God in order to follow your own teachings.
"O hypocrites! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you, for he said:
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They do not teach anything but human precepts." (Isaiah 29:13).
Jesus called out to the crowd and said to them, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
And after that, his disciples came to him. And they asked him: "Are you aware that the Pharisees were upset when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants that my Father in heaven has not planted. They will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
Haven't you understood yet? Ask them, Jesus did.
Don't you see? Everything that goes into the mouth goes into the stomach. And then it comes out of the body.
But the words that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false witness, and lying to others.
Those are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." The faith of a Canaanite woman
Jesus went out from Galilee and entered the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a woman from Canaan who was living near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is severely possessed by a demon."
Jesus didn't say a word. His disciples came to him and begged, "Send her away. She keeps crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Master!" she said. "Help me!"
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
Yes, sir, she said. But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table.
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you request." And her daughter was healed at that very moment. Jesus fed four thousand people
Jesus walked from there. And he walked by the Sea of Galilee. And he went up on the mountain and sat down.
They brought him blind people, people who couldn't walk, people who were even paralyzed, people who couldn't speak, and many others. They put them all in front of his feet, and he was standing there all by himself.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind began to see. So the people glorified the God of Israel.
And after that, Jesus called his disciples to come to him. And he said: "I feel great concern for these people. They have been with me for three days. They have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do that, they will faint on their way home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here. Where can we find enough bread to satisfy such a large crowd?"
How many loaves have you? asked Jesus. And they answered: Seven, and a few small fish.
Jesus said to the multitudes to sit down on the ground.
He took the seven loaves and the fish, and gave thanks to God. Then he broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples distributed the food to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. And afterward the disciples gathered seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat and went to the region near Magadan.
Jesus was asked to perform a miracle as proof.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus, and they asked him to show them a sign from heaven that would demonstrate his miraculous power.
He replied, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And when you see the morning, you say, "Today there will be a storm, the sky is red and overcast with clouds." You know what it means to see what is happening in the sky. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"Indeed, an evil generation of unbelievers seeks a miraculous sign. But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." Then Jesus left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The students walked to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to bring bread.
Jesus said to them, "Be on your guard. Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils discussed this among themselves. And they said: "He must have said this because we did not bring bread with us."
And Jesus knew what they were saying. And he said to them: "Your faith is very weak! Why are you talking to each other that you do not have bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you think about those five loaves of bread that were for five thousand people? Don't you think about how many baskets you filled with the pieces you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves that were among the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you picked up?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
And so the disciples understood that Jesus was not asking them to beware of the yeast used in bread, but rather He was warning them about what was being taught by the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus walked into the region of Caesarea Philippi. And he asked his disciples there: "What are people saying about the Son of Man?"
They answered: Some say John the Baptist; some, Elijah; and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets.
But what about you? he asked. Who do you say that I am?
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
This is what I say to you: You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church. And the gates of Hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And after warning Jesus's disciples not to tell anyone that he is the Messiah, Jesus speaks about his coming death.
And from that time, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He said to them, "It is necessary for me to go to Jerusalem, and there the Son of Man will suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests, and the scribes. He must be killed, and on the third day he will rise from the dead."
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. You do not think about the things of God, but about the things of people."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What benefit does a person gain if they win the whole world but lose themselves? Or what can a person exchange for their own self?
Giggi, the son of man, was born in the glory of his father. And with him will come his angels. And he will reward each one according to what he has done.
What I am going to tell you now is true. Some of the people standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' features changed upon his appearance.
After six days, Jesus took with him Peter and James and John, the brother of James. And he brought them up to a high mountain, and they were alone.
And there before them, his appearance changed. So his face shone like the sun. And his clothes became white as light.
And it came to pass, that at that moment Moses and Elijah appeared to them. And Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three shelters. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while Peter was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him!"
And when they heard this thing, the students, they were very afraid. They fell on their faces on the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them and said, "Arise, do not be afraid."
And when they lifted up their eyes, they saw no one except Jesus alone.
They came down the mountain. And while they were going down the mountain, Jesus told them what to do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make everything new again."
But I tell you: Elijah has come, and they have not recognized him, but have done to him as they pleased. And so also will the Son of Man be done to by them.
The disciples came to understand that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
And when they came near the crowd, a man came to Jesus. And he knelt down before him.
He said, "O Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is shaking violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water."
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
Jesus answered them, "O generation of vipers, how can you speak good things, for out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks? Why do you think evil in your hearts
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. And immediately, the boy was healed.
And afterward, Jesus' disciples came to him in a desert place. And they said to him, "Why could we not cast out that demon?"
He answered: "Because you have so little faith. What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, nothing will be impossible for you."
Translate
They met in Galilee. And afterward Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." And after this speech, the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus was paying the temple tax.
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Doesn't your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. And when Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth collect taxes and duties? Do they collect it from their sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. Jesus said to him, "Then the children ought not to pay."
But we don't want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tax for you and me.
